# Line endings are LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# This is not housekeeping. Git on this machine has core.autocrlf=true set at
# system level, so it rewrites LF to CRLF whenever it writes a file — which
# means a checkout can change the bytes of a file whose content nobody
# touched. build.mjs makes the asset cache-buster by hashing the bytes of
# fonts.css, styles.css, pages.css, app.js and the shared modules, so a
# checkout that flipped line endings changed the stamp on every page while
# the code was identical. That is what happened when the wholesale pivot was
# reverted: 25 files came back as CRLF, app.js among them, and the next
# build renamed every asset URL for no reason.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf for this repository, so the files git
# writes match the files the build reads, on any machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fonts are binary: never touch them.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Images, if any are ever committed rather than hotlinked.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
